(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["strings",{"l:strings/python-strings-real-costs-counts-and-canonical-s":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A Python \u003ccode>str\u003c/code> is an immutable array of code points, so indexing and \u003ccode>len\u003c/code> are O(1) but every slice, \u003ccode>+\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>replace\u003c/code> builds a new string. Most \"string problems\" are really counting, window, two-pointer, DP or parsing problems; the genuinely string-specific tools are in the next cards. For \"are these the same up to rearrangement or relabelling?\" compare \u003cb>counts\u003c/b> or a \u003cb>canonical shape\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Storage\u003c/b> (PEP 393): each string stores every code point in 1, 2 or 4 bytes, chosen by its widest character. \u003ccode>s[i]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>len(s)\u003c/code> are O(1); \u003ccode>len\u003c/code> counts code points, not bytes or visible characters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Immutability\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>s[i:j]\u003c/code> copies j - i characters. \u003ccode>res += piece\u003c/code> in a loop is O(total^2) in general (CPython sometimes extends in place when nothing else references the string; do not rely on it). Collect pieces in a list and \u003ccode>\"\".join\u003c/code> them once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hashing\u003c/b>: a string's hash is computed once and cached in the object, so repeated dict lookups with the same key object are cheap; equality is O(L) at worst.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Searching\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>in\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>find\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>count\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>replace\u003c/code> use CPython's fastsearch, a Boyer-Moore-Horspool hybrid that skips ahead on mismatches, switching to the Two-Way algorithm for long needles so the worst case stays linear. For one search, \u003ccode>pat in text\u003c/code> beats any hand-written loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>collections.Counter(s)\u003c/code> counts in C; for lowercase ASCII a 26-slot list indexed by \u003ccode>ord(ch) - 97\u003c/code> is smaller and lets you compare counts in O(26).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>The statement says\u003c/th>\u003cth>Usually means\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>anagram, same letters, rearrange\u003c/td>\u003ctd>counts\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>longest or shortest substring with a condition\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sliding window\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>two strings, subsequence, edits\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DP on prefixes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>nested brackets, encoded repeats, expressions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>stack or parser\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>palindromic substring\u003c/td>\u003ctd>expand around centres\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>find, occurrences, period, prefix equals suffix\u003c/td>\u003ctd>prefix function, Z-function or rolling hash\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\n\ndef counts26(s):\n    \"\"\"Letter counts for lowercase ASCII; compare two of these in O(26).\"\"\"\n    c = [0] * 26\n    for ch in s:\n        c[ord(ch) - 97] += 1\n    return c\n\n\ndef shape(s):\n    \"\"\"First-occurrence pattern: equal shapes mean a one-to-one letter mapping exists.\"\"\"\n    first = {}\n    return tuple(first.setdefault(ch, len(first)) for ch in s)\n\n\ndef same_letters(a, b):\n    return Counter(a) == Counter(b)\n\n\ndef build(pieces):\n    return \"\".join(pieces)             # one O(total) copy, never += in a loop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Is there a one-to-one letter mapping from \"noon\" to \"deed\", and from \"noon\" to \"dead\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">shape(\"noon\"): n is new (0), o is new (1), o seen (1), n seen (0)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>noon  -&gt;  (\u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>, 1, 0)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">shape(\"deed\"): d is 0, e is 1, e again 1, d again 0. Same shape, so n-&gt;d, o-&gt;e works both ways\u003c/p>\u003cpre>deed  -&gt;  (0, 1, 1, 0)      equal to noon's shape\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">shape(\"dead\"): d 0, e 1, a is new (2), d 0. The shapes differ at position 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>dead  -&gt;  (0, 1, \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>, 0)      noon has 1 here: o would map to e and a\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Quadratic building\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>s = s[:i] + c + s[i+1:]\u003c/code> inside a loop. Use a list of characters, then join.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One-way mappings\u003c/b>: checking only s to t lets \"ab\" map to \"cc\". Shapes, or two dicts, check both directions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>ASCII assumptions\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>ord(ch) - 97\u003c/code> breaks on capitals, digits and accents. Normalise with \u003ccode>casefold()\u003c/code>, and use \u003ccode>unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", s)\u003c/code> when \"é\" may arrive as one code point or two.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Identity\u003c/b>: compare strings with \u003ccode>==\u003c/code>, never \u003ccode>is\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is shape(\"abca\"), and is there a one-to-one mapping from \"abca\" to \"zyxz\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(0, 1, 2, 0). \"zyxz\" also has shape (0, 1, 2, 0), so yes: a to z, b to y, c to x.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know what Python string operations really cost and how counts and canonical shapes answer equivalence questions in O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:strings/palindromes-expanding-around-2n-1-centres":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every palindrome is symmetric around a \u003cb>centre\u003c/b>: a character (odd length) or the gap between two characters (even length), so a string of length n has \u003cb>2n - 1 centres\u003c/b>. From each centre, grow outwards while the two ends match. The maximal palindrome at each centre gives the longest one, and every successful growth step is one more palindrome, so the same loop also counts them.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why expanding is enough\u003c/b>: if s[lo..hi] is a palindrome, so is s[lo+1..hi-1], with the same centre. The palindromes around one centre are therefore nested, and growing until the first mismatch finds all of them: a maximal span of length L contains (L + 1) // 2 palindromes centred there.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Cost\u003c/b>: each centre costs O(1 + radius). The total is O(n^2) in the worst case (a string of one repeated letter, where every centre grows to the nearest edge) and close to O(n) on typical text, with O(1) extra space. Compare: checking all O(n^2) substrings with \u003ccode>t == t[::-1]\u003c/code> costs O(n^3); a DP table of \"is s[i..j] a palindrome\" costs O(n^2) time \u003cb>and\u003c/b> O(n^2) memory.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Manacher in outline\u003c/b>: keep the palindrome that reaches furthest right, [l, r]. For a new centre i inside it, the mirror centre j = l + r - i has already been expanded, and inside [l, r] the text around i mirrors the text around j, so i's radius is at least min(radius[j], r - i). Start expanding from there; every comparison that succeeds pushes r to the right, and r never moves left, so the total work is O(n). Interleaving a separator (\u003ccode>#a#b#a#\u003c/code>) turns every centre into a character so one loop handles both parities.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a whole string, \u003ccode>s == s[::-1]\u003c/code> is the fastest check in Python (C speed). Use two pointers when characters must be skipped or one deletion is allowed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def expand(s, lo, hi):\n    \"\"\"Grow around the centre (lo, hi) while the ends match; return the widest palindrome as (lo, hi).\"\"\"\n    while lo &gt;= 0 and hi &lt; len(s) and s[lo] == s[hi]:\n        lo -= 1\n        hi += 1\n    return lo + 1, hi - 1                 # an empty span (hi &lt; lo) when even and s[lo] != s[hi]\n\n\ndef centre_spans(s):\n    \"\"\"The maximal palindrome around each of the 2n - 1 centres.\"\"\"\n    for c in range(len(s)):\n        yield expand(s, c, c)             # odd length: centred on s[c]\n        if c + 1 &lt; len(s):\n            yield expand(s, c, c + 1)     # even length: centred on the gap after s[c]\n\n\nspans = list(centre_spans(\"cbbcabac\"))\nlo, hi = max(spans, key=lambda span: span[1] - span[0])    # longest: s[3:8] == \"cabac\"\ncount = sum((hi - lo + 2) // 2 for lo, hi in spans)        # all palindromic substrings: 12\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>s = \"cbbcabac\" (indices 0 to 7). Five of the fifteen centres, including the two that matter.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Centre on s[0] = \"c\": s[-1] does not exist, so the span is just \"c\": 1 palindrome\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns      \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> b b c a b a c\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Gap between s[1] and s[2]: b == b gives \"bb\"; then s[0] == s[3] (c == c) gives \"cbbc\"; then the left end runs out\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns      \u003cmark>c b b c\u003c/mark> a b a c      2 palindromes: bb, cbbc\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Gap between s[2] and s[3]: b != c, so nothing: 0 palindromes\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns       c b \u003cmark>b c\u003c/mark> a b a c\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Centre on s[5] = \"b\": a == a gives \"aba\", c == c gives \"cabac\", then s[2] = b vs s[8]: out of range\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns       c b b \u003cmark>c a b a c\u003c/mark>      3 palindromes: b, aba, cabac\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">After all 15 centres: longest is \"cabac\" (3..7); count = 8 single letters + bb + cbbc + aba + cabac = 12\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns       c b b \u003cmark>c a b a c\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Odd centres only\u003c/b>: misses \"bb\" and \"cbbc\". Every problem needs both parities (or Manacher's separator trick).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Off by one on return\u003c/b>: the loop exits one step past the last match, so return (lo + 1, hi - 1).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Slicing inside the loop\u003c/b>: building s[lo:hi+1] at every step turns O(n^2) into O(n^3); keep indices and slice once at the end.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Palindromic subsequence is different\u003c/b>: characters need not be adjacent, which is an interval DP, not centre expansion.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How many palindromic substrings does \"aaa\" have, and which centres produce them?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Six. Odd centres give a, a, a and \"aaa\" (the middle centre expands twice); the two gaps give \"aa\" each.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to find and count palindromic substrings by expanding around 2n - 1 centres, and why Manacher's mirror trick makes it linear.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:strings/rolling-hash-and-rabin-karp":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Treat a string as a number in base B: hash(c0 c1 ... c(L-1)) = c0 * B^(L-1) + ... + c(L-1), modulo a large prime M. Sliding the window one character costs O(1): remove the leading character's term, multiply by B, add the new character. With \u003cb>prefix hashes\u003c/b> any substring's hash is O(1) after O(n) preprocessing. Equal strings always have equal hashes; unequal strings collide only rarely if M is large and B is \u003cb>random\u003c/b>, so compare the actual text on every hit and the answer is exact.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Sliding\u003c/b>: if h is the hash of s[i:i+L], then the hash of s[i+1:i+L+1] is ((h - s[i] * B^(L-1)) * B + s[i+L]) mod M. \u003cb>Prefix hashes\u003c/b>: with H[0] = 0 and H[i+1] = H[i] * B + s[i], the hash of s[i:j] is H[j] - H[i] * B^(j-i) (mod M).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Collisions\u003c/b>: for two different strings of length L and a base chosen uniformly at random, a collision means B is a root of a nonzero polynomial of degree below L, which has fewer than L roots, so the probability is below L / M. With M = 2^61 - 1 (a Mersenne prime) and L = 10^5 that is under 10^-13 per comparison. Storing N hashes in a set is a birthday problem: expect about N^2 / (2M) colliding pairs, which is about 500 for N = 10^6 and M near 10^9. Hence: a 61-bit prime (Python's big integers make this easy), or \u003cb>double hashing\u003c/b> (two independent moduli and bases, standard in C++ and Java where 64-bit products overflow); a base drawn at run time, so nobody can craft colliding inputs (with the \"free\" modulus 2^64, a known family of strings built from the Thue-Morse sequence collides for every base); and a real comparison before reporting a match.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Cost\u003c/b>: O(n + m) expected for Rabin-Karp, but every true match is verified in O(m), so \"aaa\" in \"aaaa...\" costs O(nm); KMP and Z guarantee O(n + m). Hashing wins when you compare \u003cb>many\u003c/b> substrings: duplicates of length L, longest repeated substring (binary search on L), equality of arbitrary ranges.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>In real tools\u003c/b>: rsync's receiver sends a weak 32-bit rolling checksum (two running sums modulo 2^16, inspired by Adler-32) and a strong checksum per block; the sender slides the weak one over \u003cb>every byte offset\u003c/b> of its file and computes the strong one only for candidates. restic cuts chunks where a Rabin fingerprint of the last 64 bytes matches a pattern, so an insertion moves only nearby boundaries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\nMOD = (1 &lt;&lt; 61) - 1                       # Mersenne prime\nBASE = random.randrange(256, MOD - 1)     # random per run: no input can be crafted against it\n\n\nclass PrefixHash:\n    \"\"\"Hash of any substring s[i:j] in O(1) after O(n) preprocessing.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, s):\n        self.h = [0] * (len(s) + 1)       # h[i] = hash of s[:i]\n        self.p = [1] * (len(s) + 1)       # p[i] = BASE**i % MOD\n        for i, ch in enumerate(s):\n            self.h[i + 1] = (self.h[i] * BASE + ord(ch)) % MOD\n            self.p[i + 1] = self.p[i] * BASE % MOD\n\n    def get(self, i, j):\n        return (self.h[j] - self.h[i] * self.p[j - i]) % MOD\n\n\ndef rabin_karp(text, pat):\n    \"\"\"All start indices of pat in text. Every hash hit is checked, so the answer is exact.\"\"\"\n    n, m = len(text), len(pat)\n    if m == 0:\n        return list(range(n + 1))\n    if m &gt; n:\n        return []\n    target = h = 0\n    for k in range(m):\n        target = (target * BASE + ord(pat[k])) % MOD\n        h = (h * BASE + ord(text[k])) % MOD\n    top = pow(BASE, m - 1, MOD)           # weight of the character that leaves the window\n    out = []\n    for i in range(n - m + 1):\n        if h == target and text[i:i + m] == pat:\n            out.append(i)\n        if i + m &lt; n:\n            h = ((h - ord(text[i]) * top) * BASE + ord(text[i + m])) % MOD\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find \"159\" in \"31415926\" with a deliberately tiny modulus: B = 10, M = 13, digits as values. hash(\"159\") = 159 mod 13 = 3, and B^2 mod 13 = 9.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Window 314: 314 mod 13 = 2, not the target 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text    \u003cmark>3 1 4\u003c/mark> 1 5 9 2 6        h = 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: drop 3, add 1: (2 - 3 * 9) * 10 + 1 = -249, and -249 mod 13 = 11 (= 141 mod 13)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text    3 \u003cmark>1 4 1\u003c/mark> 5 9 2 6        h = 11\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: (11 - 1 * 9) * 10 + 5 = 25, mod 13 = 12 (= 415 mod 13)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text    3 1 \u003cmark>4 1 5\u003c/mark> 9 2 6        h = 12\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide: (12 - 4 * 9) * 10 + 9 = -231, mod 13 = 3: a hit. Compare the text: \"159\" == \"159\", match at 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text    3 1 4 \u003cmark>1 5 9\u003c/mark> 2 6        h = \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slide twice more: 592 gives 7, then 926 gives 3: another hit, but \"926\" != \"159\". A collision, caught by the comparison\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text    3 1 4 1 5 \u003cmark>9 2 6\u003c/mark>        h = \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   rejected\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With M = 2^61 - 1 and a random base, that second hit would essentially never happen; the comparison makes the answer exact either way.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Negative remainders\u003c/b> in C++, Java or Go: \u003ccode>(a - b) % M\u003c/code> can be negative; add M before taking the remainder. Python's \u003ccode>%\u003c/code> is already non-negative.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overflow\u003c/b>: in 64-bit languages the product of two values below 2^61 overflows; use 128-bit multiplication or two 31-bit moduli.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Skipping verification\u003c/b>, or using a small or famous modulus with a fixed base on adversarial input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Comparing hashes of different lengths\u003c/b>: with prefix hashes, \"a\" and \"\\x00a\" can collide; only compare equal-length substrings, or include the length in the key.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Python's \u003ccode>hash()\u003c/code>\u003c/b> of a slice still costs O(L), and it is randomised per process: fine within one run, never for values you store.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must the base be random, and what does verifying each hit cost in the worst case?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With a fixed base (and modulus), someone can compute two different strings with equal hashes in advance; a base chosen at run time makes any fixed pair collide with probability below L / M. Verification costs O(m) per hit, so a text full of true matches costs O(nm) in total.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to slide and query polynomial hashes in O(1), why a 61-bit prime with a random base is safe, and why every hit is verified.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:strings/kmp-and-the-prefix-function":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>pi[i] is the length of the longest \u003cb>border\u003c/b> of s[:i+1]: a proper prefix that is also a suffix. When a match of k characters fails, the pattern can resume at pi[k-1] characters instead of starting over, because that much of what you just matched is already a prefix of the pattern. The text pointer never moves backwards, so matching costs O(n + m), and pi itself is built by the same loop in O(m).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Building pi\u003c/b>: keep k = pi[i-1], the border you are trying to extend. If s[i] == s[k], the border grows to k + 1. If not, fall back to the next shorter border of the same prefix, which is pi[k-1], and try again; stop at k = 0. The borders of a string form a chain k, pi[k-1], pi[pi[k-1]-1], ..., so the fallback visits every candidate in decreasing order and the first that extends is the longest.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why O(m)\u003c/b>: k rises by at most 1 per index and every fallback lowers it by at least 1, so the total number of fallbacks is at most the total number of rises: at most m. The same argument bounds matching by O(n + m).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Matching\u003c/b>: run the identical loop with the text as input and the pattern as the thing being extended; when k reaches m, report a match at i - m + 1 and fall back to pi[m-1] so overlapping matches are found. Equivalently, compute pi of pattern + \"\\x00\" + text and report every position where pi equals m (the separator must not occur in either string).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>What else pi tells you\u003c/b>: s[:pi[n-1]] is the longest prefix that is also a suffix. p = n - pi[n-1] is the smallest period, and s is a whole repetition of a block exactly when pi[n-1] &gt; 0 and n % p == 0. The longest palindromic prefix of s is pi's last value for s + \"#\" + reversed(s).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>In real tools\u003c/b>: KMP is a worst-case guarantee and a source of structure, not what fast searchers run. CPython's \u003ccode>str.find\u003c/code> and GNU grep skip ahead with Boyer-Moore-style shift tables (CPython switches to Two-Way for long needles). Aho-Corasick is KMP generalised to a trie of many patterns, with failure links playing the role of pi; Suricata offers it as a multi-pattern matcher.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def prefix_function(s):\n    \"\"\"pi[i] = length of the longest proper prefix of s[:i + 1] that is also its suffix.\"\"\"\n    pi = [0] * len(s)\n    k = 0                                  # the border being extended\n    for i in range(1, len(s)):\n        while k &gt; 0 and s[i] != s[k]:\n            k = pi[k - 1]                  # next shorter border\n        if s[i] == s[k]:\n            k += 1\n        pi[i] = k\n    return pi\n\n\ndef kmp_search(text, pat):\n    \"\"\"All start indices of pat in text in O(n + m).\"\"\"\n    if not pat:\n        return list(range(len(text) + 1))\n    pi = prefix_function(pat)\n    out, k = [], 0                         # k = characters of pat matched so far\n    for i, ch in enumerate(text):\n        while k &gt; 0 and ch != pat[k]:\n            k = pi[k - 1]\n        if ch == pat[k]:\n            k += 1\n        if k == len(pat):\n            out.append(i - k + 1)\n            k = pi[k - 1]                  # keep going: matches may overlap\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build pi for the pattern \"abacabab\", then match it in \"abacabacabab\".\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1, 2: \"b\" cannot extend the empty border (pi 0); \"a\" matches s[0] (pi 1)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s    a b a c a b a b\npi   0 0 \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3: \"c\" != s[1] = \"b\", fall back to k = pi[0] = 0; \"c\" != s[0]: pi 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s    a b a \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> a b a b\npi   0 0 1 \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4, 5, 6: a, b, a each extend the border: \"a\", \"ab\", \"aba\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s    a b a c \u003cmark>a b a\u003c/mark> b\npi   0 0 1 0 \u003cmark>1 2 3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 7: \"b\" != s[3] = \"c\", so fall back to k = pi[2] = 1; \"b\" == s[1]: k = 2. The border is \"ab\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s    a b a c a b a \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>\npi   0 0 1 0 1 2 3 \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Matching: text[0..6] = \"abacaba\" equals the pattern's first 7 characters: k = 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text  \u003cmark>a b a c a b a\u003c/mark> c a b a b\npat   \u003cmark>a b a c a b a\u003c/mark> b\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">text[7] = \"c\" but pat[7] = \"b\": mismatch. Fall back to k = pi[6] = 3, since \"aba\" is both a suffix of what matched and a prefix of pat\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text  a b a c \u003cmark>a b a\u003c/mark> c a b a b\npat           \u003cmark>a b a\u003c/mark> c a b a b\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Compare the same text character again: text[7] = \"c\" == pat[3]: k = 4. The text pointer never went back\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text  a b a c a b a \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> a b a b\npat           a b a \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> a b a b\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">text[8..11] = \"abab\" extend k to 8 = m: match at 11 - 8 + 1 = 4, then k = pi[7] = 2 for overlapping matches\u003c/p>\u003cpre>text  a b a c \u003cmark>a b a c a b a b\u003c/mark>\npat           \u003cmark>a b a c a b a b\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>k = pi[k] instead of pi[k - 1]\u003c/b>: pi is indexed by the last position of the matched prefix, which is k - 1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>if\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>while\u003c/code>\u003c/b> for the fallback: one step back is not always enough.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No fallback after a full match\u003c/b>: the next comparison reads pat[m] and raises IndexError, or overlapping matches are lost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A separator that occurs in the data\u003c/b> when using pattern + separator + text; pick a character outside the alphabet, or run the two-phase version above.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the prefix function of \"aabaaab\", and what are its smallest period and longest border?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>[0, 1, 0, 1, 2, 2, 3]. The longest border is \"aab\" (length 3), so the smallest period is 7 - 3 = 4; since 7 % 4 != 0, the string is not a whole repetition of a block.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to build the prefix function with its fallback chain, match in O(n + m), and read borders and periods from it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:strings/the-z-function-and-its-z-box":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>z[i] is the length of the longest common prefix of s and the suffix starting at i. The algorithm keeps the \u003cb>Z-box\u003c/b> [l, r): the rightmost segment known to equal a prefix of s. For an i inside the box, s[i:r] is a copy of s[i-l:r-l], so z[i] starts at min(r - i, z[i - l]) for free; only characters beyond r are compared, and r never moves left, so the whole array costs O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Three cases\u003c/b> for each i from 1 to n - 1:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>i is outside the box\u003c/b> (i &gt;= r): compare from scratch, s[0] with s[i], s[1] with s[i+1], ...\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inside, and the copied value fits\u003c/b> (z[i - l] &lt; r - i): the answer is exactly z[i - l]; the mismatch that stopped it is also inside the box. No comparisons succeed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inside, and the copy reaches the box edge\u003c/b> (z[i - l] &gt;= r - i): start at r - i and keep comparing past r.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Whenever the match for i ends beyond r, the box becomes [i, i + z[i]).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why O(n)\u003c/b>: every successful comparison moves r one step to the right (it compares s at a position at or beyond r), r only grows, and it stops at n. Each i adds at most one failed comparison. Total: O(n).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Uses\u003c/b>: matching (compute z of pattern + separator + text; a position with z equal to m is a match); the sum of all z values plus n is the total length of all prefix-suffix matches (LeetCode 2223); a period p of s exists where p + z[p] == n. The prefix function and the Z-function carry the same information and can be converted into each other in O(n); many people find the Z-box easier to reason about, while KMP's form streams naturally (it never needs the text in advance).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def z_function(s):\n    \"\"\"z[i] = length of the longest common prefix of s and s[i:]; z[0] is left as 0.\"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    z = [0] * n\n    l = r = 0                                  # Z-box: s[l:r] == s[0:r - l]\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if i &lt; r:\n            z[i] = min(r - i, z[i - l])        # copy from inside the box\n        while i + z[i] &lt; n and s[z[i]] == s[i + z[i]]:\n            z[i] += 1                          # compare only past what is known\n        if i + z[i] &gt; r:\n            l, r = i, i + z[i]\n    return z\n\n\ndef z_search(text, pat, sep=\"\\x00\"):\n    \"\"\"Start indices of pat in text; sep must occur in neither string.\"\"\"\n    z = z_function(pat + sep + text)\n    m = len(pat)\n    return [i - m - 1 for i in range(m + 1, len(z)) if z[i] &gt;= m]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>s = \"abaababc\". Box [l, r) shown with brackets below the string.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1, 2 are outside any box: \"b\" vs \"a\" fails (z 0); \"a\" matches s[0], then \"a\" vs \"b\" fails (z 1). Box [2, 3)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i     0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ns     a b a a b a b c\nz     0 0 \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3: outside the box. \"aba\" matches s[0:3], then s[6] = \"b\" vs s[3] = \"a\" fails: z = 3, box [3, 6)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s     a b a \u003cmark>a b a\u003c/mark> b c\nbox         [3     6)\nz     0 0 1 \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4: inside. Copy z[4 - 3] = z[1] = 0, which is less than r - i = 2: z = 0 with no comparisons\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s     a b a a \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark> a b c\nbox         [3     6)\nz     0 0 1 3 \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 5: inside. z[5 - 3] = z[2] = 1 reaches the box edge (r - i = 1), so start at 1 and compare past r: s[1] = \"b\" == s[6], then \"a\" vs \"c\" fails. z = 2, box [5, 7)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s     a b a a b \u003cmark>a b\u003c/mark> c\nbox               [5  7)\nz     0 0 1 3 0 \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 6: inside. z[1] = 0 &lt; r - i = 1, so z = 0. i = 7: outside, \"c\" vs \"a\" fails, z = 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>s     a b a a b a \u003cmark>b c\u003c/mark>\nz     0 0 1 3 0 2 \u003cmark>0 0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the min with r - i\u003c/b>: copying z[i - l] blindly claims matches beyond the box that were never checked.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Updating the box unconditionally\u003c/b>: when i + z[i] does not pass r, replacing the box moves r to the left, forgets matched text and breaks the O(n) argument. Update only when i + z[i] &gt; r.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>z[0]\u003c/b>: the template leaves it 0; some problems want n there (2223 counts the whole string), so add it explicitly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The separator\u003c/b>: as with KMP, it must not occur in the pattern or text, and searching with \u003ccode>z[i] &gt;= m\u003c/code> relies on it to stop matches at length m.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">For s = \"aaaa\", what is the Z-array, and how many character comparisons succeed in total?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>[0, 3, 2, 1]. Only i = 1 compares from scratch (3 successes, then the end of the string); i = 2 and i = 3 lie inside the box [1, 4) and copy min(r - i, z[i - l]) = 2 and 1 without any successful comparison. Three successes in total.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how the Z-box lets you compute every prefix-match length in O(n), and how to use it for matching.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:strings/parsing-tokenising-and-length-prefixed-encodings":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Parse in two layers: a \u003cb>tokeniser\u003c/b> turns characters into tokens (numbers, words, symbols), and a parser or \u003cb>state machine\u003c/b> consumes tokens with an explicit state such as \"before sign, in digits, done\". To store a list of strings in one string, a delimiter alone fails as soon as a value contains the delimiter; prefix each value with its \u003cb>length\u003c/b> (or escape the delimiter) and decoding becomes unambiguous.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Tokenise first.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>s.split()\u003c/code> (no argument) splits on runs of whitespace and drops empty strings, which is exactly \"words\" for most problems. For richer input, one regular expression with named alternatives, scanned with \u003ccode>re.finditer\u003c/code>, yields typed tokens in one pass; a hand-written index loop does the same with full control. Each character is examined a constant number of times, so tokenising is O(n).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>State machines.\u003c/b> Write the states down before coding: for a signed integer they are leading spaces, optional sign, digits, stop. Each character either moves to the next state or ends the parse. Clamping, overflow and \"stop at the first non-digit\" then become explicit transitions instead of scattered ifs. Nested formats (brackets, \u003ccode>3[ab]\u003c/code>-style repeats) add a stack of partial results.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Run-length encoding\u003c/b> replaces each maximal run of a character by (character, count): \"aaabcc\" becomes a3 b1 c2. One pass, O(n). The look-and-say sequence is RLE applied to the previous term, read aloud.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why delimiters fail.\u003c/b> Join [\"a,b\", \"c\"] with \",\" and you get \"a,b,c\", which decodes to three values. Either escape the delimiter inside values (CSV doubles quotes; JSON escapes backslashes and quotes), or write each value's \u003cb>length\u003c/b> first. Real formats do the second: HTTP bodies carry Content-Length, Redis's protocol sends bulk strings as \u003ccode>$&lt;length&gt;\\r\\n&lt;data&gt;\\r\\n\u003c/code>, and Protocol Buffers prefix strings and nested messages with a varint length. A length prefix needs no scanning of the data, so it handles binary content.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import re\n\nTOKEN = re.compile(r\"\\s*(?:(?P&lt;num&gt;\\d+)|(?P&lt;name&gt;[A-Za-z_]\\w*)|(?P&lt;op&gt;\\S))\")\n\n\ndef tokens(text):\n    \"\"\"[(kind, value), ...] for numbers, names and single-character operators.\"\"\"\n    out = []\n    for m in TOKEN.finditer(text):\n        kind = m.lastgroup\n        if kind:                               # None only for trailing whitespace\n            out.append((kind, m.group(kind)))\n    return out\n\n\ndef rle(s):\n    \"\"\"'aaabcc' -&gt; [('a', 3), ('b', 1), ('c', 2)]\"\"\"\n    runs = []\n    for ch in s:\n        if runs and runs[-1][0] == ch:\n            runs[-1][1] += 1\n        else:\n            runs.append([ch, 1])\n    return [(ch, n) for ch, n in runs]\n\n\ndef encode(strings):\n    \"\"\"Length-prefixed: any characters, including ':' and digits, survive a round trip.\"\"\"\n    return \"\".join(f\"{len(s)}:{s}\" for s in strings)\n\n\ndef decode(data):\n    out, i = [], 0\n    while i &lt; len(data):\n        j = data.index(\":\", i)                 # the length field itself never contains ':'\n        n = int(data[i:j])\n        out.append(data[j + 1:j + 1 + n])\n        i = j + 1 + n\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>encode([\"a:b\", \"\", \"5:x\"])\u003c/code> is \"3:a:b0:3:5:x\". Decoding never looks inside a value, so the colons and digits in the data are harmless.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 0: the first \":\" after i is at 1, so the length is \"3\"; take the 3 characters after it: \"a:b\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>data  \u003cmark>3:\u003c/mark>\u003cmark>a:b\u003c/mark>0:3:5:x\nout   [\"a:b\"]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 5: \":\" at 6, length \"0\": an empty string, and the next field starts at 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>data  3:a:b\u003cmark>0:\u003c/mark>3:5:x\nout   [\"a:b\", \"\"]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 7: \":\" at 8, length \"3\": take \"5:x\". i = 12 = len(data): done\u003c/p>\u003cpre>data  3:a:b0:\u003cmark>3:\u003c/mark>\u003cmark>5:x\u003c/mark>\nout   [\"a:b\", \"\", \"5:x\"]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>split(\" \")\u003c/code> versus \u003ccode>split()\u003c/code>\u003c/b>: the first keeps empty strings between repeated spaces; the second collapses whitespace.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unclear states\u003c/b>: \"  +-12\" and \"-\" are where ad hoc parsers break. List the states and test every transition, including empty input and a lone sign.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overflow in other languages\u003c/b>: accumulate with a check before multiplying by 10 (Python's ints never overflow, so clamp explicitly when the problem asks for 32-bit results).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Trusting a length field\u003c/b> from untrusted input: check it against the remaining data before slicing, or a corrupted prefix silently swallows the next value.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does decoding \"3:a:b0:3:5:x\" never misread the \":\" inside \"a:b\" or \"5:x\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The decoder searches for \":\" only at the start of a field, to end the length; it then jumps over exactly n characters without inspecting them. Characters inside a value are never treated as structure.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to tokenise before parsing, model a parser as states, run-length encode, and encode lists so that any content round-trips.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:strings/find-the-duplicates-repeated-passages-across-doc":"\u003cp>Plagiarism checkers, deduplicating backup tools and near-duplicate detectors all ask the same question: which passages of length L appear in more than one document? You will answer it three ways on about 420,000 characters of synthetic documents with planted copies, check that all three agree exactly, then measure time and memory for L = 50, 500 and 2,000. The point is not only \"O(n) beats O(nL)\", but where that crossover really is in CPython, and what collisions cost.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10 or newer; no packages needed. The run takes about two minutes, most of it the memory measurements (tracemalloc slows allocation down).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>dup_lab.py\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>by_slices\u003c/code> is the exact baseline (every window as a dict key). The two hash-based methods store one integer per window and \u003cb>verify\u003c/b> every hit against the real text, so a collision can cost time but never a wrong answer.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\"\"\"Find passages of L characters that appear in two different documents: exact slices, hashed slices, rolling hash.\"\"\"\nimport random\nimport time\nimport tracemalloc\n\nMOD = (1 &lt;&lt; 61) - 1                      # Mersenne prime modulus\nBASE = random.randrange(256, MOD - 1)    # random base, chosen once per run\n\n\ndef make_docs(n_docs=30, words=2_000, plants=30, seed=7):\n    \"\"\"Random-word documents plus planted copies of 60-3000 character passages.\"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    vocab = [\"\".join(rng.choices(\"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\", k=rng.randint(2, 9))) for _ in range(5_000)]\n    docs = [\" \".join(rng.choices(vocab, k=words)) for _ in range(n_docs)]\n    for _ in range(plants):\n        a, b = rng.sample(range(n_docs), 2)\n        size = rng.choice((60, 300, 3_000))\n        start = rng.randrange(len(docs[a]) - size)\n        pos = rng.randrange(len(docs[b]))\n        docs[b] = docs[b][:pos] + docs[a][start:start + size] + docs[b][pos:]\n    return docs\n\n\ndef by_slices(docs, L):\n    \"\"\"Exact baseline: every window is a dict key. Returns the set of (first doc, later doc) pairs.\"\"\"\n    first, pairs = {}, set()\n    for d, text in enumerate(docs):\n        for i in range(len(text) - L + 1):\n            owner = first.setdefault(text[i:i + L], d)\n            if owner != d:\n                pairs.add((owner, d))\n    return pairs\n\n\ndef _check(buckets, docs, L, key, d, i, pairs):\n    \"\"\"A hash hit. buckets[key] holds the first (doc &lt;&lt; 32 | start) of each distinct window with this hash.\n    Compare the real text, so a collision costs time but never a wrong answer. Returns 1 for a collision.\"\"\"\n    window = docs[d][i:i + L]\n    entry = buckets[key]\n    entries = entry if isinstance(entry, list) else [entry]\n    for packed in entries:\n        od, oi = packed &gt;&gt; 32, packed &amp; 0xFFFFFFFF\n        if docs[od][oi:oi + L] == window:\n            if od != d:\n                pairs.add((od, d))\n            return 0\n    buckets[key] = entries + [d &lt;&lt; 32 | i]          # same hash, different text\n    return 1\n\n\ndef by_slice_hash(docs, L):\n    buckets, pairs, collisions = {}, set(), 0\n    for d, text in enumerate(docs):\n        for i in range(len(text) - L + 1):\n            key = hash(text[i:i + L])                   # O(L): slice, then hash, both in C\n            if key in buckets:\n                collisions += _check(buckets, docs, L, key, d, i, pairs)\n            else:\n                buckets[key] = d &lt;&lt; 32 | i\n    return pairs, collisions\n\n\ndef by_rolling(docs, L, mod=MOD):\n    buckets, pairs, collisions = {}, set(), 0\n    top = pow(BASE, L - 1, mod)\n    for d, text in enumerate(docs):\n        if len(text) &lt; L:\n            continue\n        codes = [ord(ch) for ch in text]\n        h = 0\n        for c in codes[:L]:\n            h = (h * BASE + c) % mod\n        for i in range(len(text) - L + 1):\n            if h in buckets:\n                collisions += _check(buckets, docs, L, h, d, i, pairs)\n            else:\n                buckets[h] = d &lt;&lt; 32 | i\n            if i + L &lt; len(text):\n                h = ((h - codes[i] * top) * BASE + codes[i + L]) % mod   # O(1) slide\n    return pairs, collisions\n\n\ndef timed(fn, *args):\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    out = fn(*args)\n    return out, time.perf_counter() - t\n\n\ndef peak_mb(fn, *args):\n    tracemalloc.start()\n    fn(*args)\n    peak = tracemalloc.get_traced_memory()[1] / 1e6\n    tracemalloc.stop()\n    return peak\n\n\ndef main():\n    docs = make_docs()\n    print(f\"{len(docs)} documents, {sum(map(len, docs)):,} characters\")\n    for L in (50, 500, 2_000):\n        print(f\"L = {L}\")\n        results = {}\n        methods = [(\"hash(slice)\", by_slice_hash, ()), (\"rolling hash\", by_rolling, ()),\n                   (\"rolling, mod 1000003\", by_rolling, (1_000_003,))]\n        if L &lt;= 500:\n            methods.insert(0, (\"dict of slices\", by_slices, ()))\n        for name, fn, extra in methods:\n            out, secs = timed(fn, docs, L, *extra)\n            results[name] = out if isinstance(out, set) else out[0]\n            note = \"\" if isinstance(out, set) else f\"  collisions {out[1]}\"\n            print(f\"  {name:21} {secs:6.2f} s  peak {peak_mb(fn, docs, L, *extra):6.1f} MB{note}\")\n        answers = list(results.values())\n        assert all(a == answers[0] for a in answers), \"methods disagree\"\n        print(f\"  all methods agree: {len(answers[0])} document pairs share a passage\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Predict before running: which method is fastest at L = 50, and at L = 2,000? Which uses the most memory at L = 500? Roughly how many collisions will the modulus 1,000,003 cause on about 420,000 windows?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>python3 dup_lab.py\u003c/code> and copy the output into a table: method by L, with time and peak memory.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Explain the L = 50 row. Slicing and hashing 50 characters runs in C and costs about as much as the handful of Python operations the rolling update needs per window, so O(nL) in C competes with O(n) in bytecode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Explain the memory column: \u003ccode>by_slices\u003c/code> keeps every window as a string of about 49 + L bytes, while the hash methods keep one integer per window whatever L is.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Explain the collision count with the birthday effect: with N windows and M buckets you expect about N^2 / (2M) colliding pairs while N is much smaller than M, and when N approaches M a large fraction of new windows land in an occupied bucket. Then confirm the answers still agree: verification removed every false hit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stretch: winnowing, the fingerprinting idea used by plagiarism detectors. Instead of storing every window hash, store only the smallest hash in each run of w consecutive window hashes. Implement it for w = 20 and check that every planted passage of at least L + w - 1 characters is still found while far fewer fingerprints are stored.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Times on a recent machine (a shared one can be twice as slow); memory is stable:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>L\u003c/th>\u003cth>dict of slices\u003c/th>\u003cth>hash(slice)\u003c/th>\u003cth>rolling hash\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>50\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.4 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.5 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.8-1.3 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.7 s, 220 MB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.5-0.7 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.8-1 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(skipped: about 0.9 GB)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 1-1.1 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 0.7-0.9 s, 55 MB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>All methods agree: about 32 document pairs share a 50-character passage and about 10 share a 500- or 2,000-character one (only the long planted copies are that long).\u003c/li>\u003cli>At L = 50 the C-level slicing methods win; the rolling hash's cost is flat in L while slicing grows with it, so the rolling hash catches up somewhere between 500 and 2,000 characters and is ahead at 2,000. In a compiled language the crossover comes far earlier.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Exact slices are fine for short L and hopeless for long L: memory grows with L, about four times larger at 500 than at 50.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With the modulus 1,000,003, expect tens of thousands of collisions, a little extra time for verification, and exactly the same answer. With 2^61 - 1, zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A service must find repeated 4 KB blocks across terabytes of backups. Which approach from this lab scales, and what would you change about it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The rolling hash: its cost per byte does not depend on the block size, and it stores one small integer per candidate instead of a 4 KB key. In production you would write it in a compiled language, store only some fingerprints (content-defined boundaries as restic does, or winnowing), keep the hash index on disk, and confirm candidates with a strong hash or a byte comparison, as rsync does.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:205":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Decide whether the letters of one string can be renamed, consistently and one-to-one, to produce another string of the same length\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A renaming must send every copy of a letter to the same letter, and two different letters may not share a target. What pattern do both strings have to share?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counts and canonical shapes\u003c/p>\u003cp>Map each character to the index of its first occurrence; two strings are related by a one-to-one renaming exactly when these first-occurrence patterns are equal. Two dicts (s to t and t to s) checked position by position work too; checking only one direction is the classic bug.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(alphabet) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:28":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Return where a pattern first occurs inside a text, or -1\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>After a partial match fails, the naive method restarts from the next text position. Could the part you already matched tell you where the pattern can resume?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix function (KMP)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Build the prefix function of the pattern; scan the text once, and on a mismatch fall back to pi[k - 1] characters matched instead of zero, so the text pointer never moves backwards. The first time k reaches m, the answer is i - m + 1. (In everyday Python, text.find(pattern) is the right tool.)\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + m) time, O(m) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:459":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Can the whole string be produced by writing some shorter substring several times in a row?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If the string is a repetition of a block, what is true of its longest proper prefix that is also a suffix?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix function (KMP)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Let b = pi[n - 1] be the longest border; then p = n - b is the smallest period, and the string is a whole repetition exactly when b &gt; 0 and n % p == 0. Equivalently, s occurs in (s + s)[1:-1]: rotating a repetition by one period gives the string back.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:5":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Return the longest contiguous piece of the string that reads the same in both directions\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every such piece is symmetric about a middle point. How many possible middles are there, and what can you do from each?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Expand around centres\u003c/p>\u003cp>Expand from each of the 2n - 1 centres (each character and each gap) while the two ends match; the widest span found wins. Each centre costs its radius, so O(n^2) in the worst case with O(1) extra space (Manacher brings it to O(n)).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^2) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:647":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count how many contiguous substrings read the same in both directions, counting equal substrings at different positions separately\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Use the same middles as for the longest one. How many palindromes does one successful expansion step add?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Expand around centres\u003c/p>\u003cp>Expand around all 2n - 1 centres; every step where the ends still match is exactly one more palindrome centred there, so the answer is the total number of successful steps (a maximal span of length L contributes (L + 1) // 2).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^2) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:151":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Reverse the order of the words in a sentence, removing leading, trailing and repeated spaces\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Treat the sentence as a list of tokens rather than characters. Which split collapses runs of spaces for you?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Tokenisers, state machines and encodings\u003c/p>\u003cp>Tokenise with split() (no argument), which drops empty strings, then join the reversed list with single spaces. Without a split, reverse the whole character buffer, then reverse each word and compact spaces with a write index.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space (O(1) extra for the in-place buffer version)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:8":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Read a signed integer from the front of a messy string and clamp it to the 32-bit range\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Write down the stages the input goes through (spaces, an optional sign, digits) and what ends each one.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Tokenisers, state machines and encodings\u003c/p>\u003cp>A small state machine: skip leading spaces, read at most one sign, then accumulate digits until the first non-digit; clamp the result to [-2^31, 2^31 - 1]. In languages with fixed-width ints, check before multiplying by 10.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:38":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each term of a sequence describes the previous term by reading out its groups of equal digits\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>How do you describe \"111221\" in one left-to-right pass? What counts as one group?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Tokenisers, state machines and encodings\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each term is the run-length encoding of the previous one, written as count then digit. Scan maximal runs, append str(count) and the digit to a list, join once, and repeat n - 1 times; the terms grow by roughly 30% each step, so build with join.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(total length of all terms generated) time\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1392":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Return the longest non-empty prefix of a string that is also a suffix, without being the whole string\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>This is precisely one value that the KMP preprocessing computes. Which one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix function (KMP)\u003c/p>\u003cp>The answer is s[:pi[n - 1]], the longest border. Alternatively compare prefix and suffix hashes for every length from longest to shortest and verify the first equal pair.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:214":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Make the string a palindrome by adding as few characters as possible, only at the front\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Whatever you add in front mirrors part of the end. Which part of the original string can stay unmatched in the middle?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix function (KMP)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Find the longest palindromic prefix of s: it is the last prefix-function value of s + \"#\" + reversed(s), since a border there is a prefix of s equal to a suffix of its reverse. Answer: reversed(s[L:]) + s.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:1044":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Find the longest substring that occurs at least twice (occurrences may overlap), for a string of up to 3 * 10^4 characters\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If some length works, every shorter length works too. How fast can you test one length?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Rolling hash\u003c/p>\u003cp>Binary search on the length L; test a length by sliding a polynomial hash over every window and checking for a repeat in a set, verifying candidates against the real text (or using a 61-bit modulus with a random base). Each test is O(n), so O(n log n) overall.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) expected time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:2223":"\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Sum, over every suffix, the length of its longest common prefix with the whole string\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each term compares the string with itself shifted by i. Which array stores exactly those lengths?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Z-function\u003c/p>\u003cp>Those terms are the Z-function: z[i] is the longest common prefix of s and s[i:]. Compute it in O(n) with the Z-box; the answer is n (for the whole string) plus the sum of z[i] for i &gt;= 1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>"}]);
